# === Identity documents — never in this repo ===
# E6-S9 needs a real Aadhaar QR to test signature verification against, and a real
# one belongs to a real person. Git history is permanent and this repo has a remote,
# so the fixtures live outside the working tree entirely (see docs/stories E6-S9).
# These patterns are a second line of defence, not the plan.
**/aadhaar*
**/aadhar*
**/*-identity-document.*
fixtures-private/

# ...but source that merely *names* the thing is not one of them, and these patterns cannot tell
# the difference. `AadhaarIdentity.java` is a record of a name, a date of birth and four digits —
# no number, no scan, nothing that belongs to anybody — and it sat untracked for two days because
# `**/aadhaar*` matched its filename. It compiled on the machine that wrote it and nowhere else,
# which is the failure mode an ignore rule over source always has: silent locally, fatal on the
# first checkout that does not already have the file.
#
# An identity document is an image, a scan or a signed payload. It is never a class.
#
# Scoped to the source trees rather than written as a blanket `!**/*.ts`: a blanket negation sits
# below every rule above it and would quietly re-include things that are ignored on purpose —
# `next-env.d.ts` among them, which Next regenerates on every build.
!backend/src/**/*.java
!frontend/app/**/*.ts
!frontend/app/**/*.tsx
!frontend/components/**/*.ts
!frontend/components/**/*.tsx
!frontend/lib/**/*.ts
!frontend/__tests__/**/*.ts
!frontend/__tests__/**/*.tsx

# `fixtures-private/` above is a directory, so these negations cannot reach inside it — git does
# not descend into an ignored directory. The real Aadhaar QR stays out either way.
